{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion bias: The model consistently forecasts a slow, linear drift toward a stable mean (approx. 80-85 EUR), failing to capture the sharp volatility and subsequent crash (dropping to ~75 EUR) observed in the true outcomes. Apply a negative correction factor of 3-5 EUR for the first 10 days of the forecast horizon when recent history shows a spike above 84 EUR.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model exhibits a significant lag in reacting to downward trends. When the 18-day history shows a peak followed by a decline (e.g., dropping from 85.30 to 80.62), the model continues to forecast an increase. Force the forecast to align with the immediate downward momentum of the last 3-5 days rather than the long-term average.",
    "Incorporate sentiment-triggered volatility: Although sentiment scores are mostly zero, the appearance of positive sentiment (0.20-0.40) coincides with the model's failure to predict the subsequent price drop. Treat any non-zero sentiment in the recent history as a signal of market instability or impending correction, requiring a reduction in the forecasted upward trajectory.",
    "Cap long-horizon forecasts: The model's 30-day forecast shows a steady, unrealistic increase. Cap the forecast at the current spot price or the recent 10-day average for horizons beyond 15 days to prevent overestimation during bearish phases."
  ]
}
